Both bakeoff priority playbooks pointed at "the 45-linker catalogue".
The catalogue was 45 at ADR-3bbb rollout, 60 for most of the interval
since, and is 61 today -- so the number had been wrong twice over, in
two files, in text loaded into the agent's context on every BROAD and
DEEP session.

Bumping 45 to 61 would only reset the clock. Both sentences already
link to docs/LINKERS.md, which carries the authoritative count beside
the table it describes, and that count is now gated: LINKERS.md's rows
are reconciled against the live linker registry, and
tests/test_generate_architecture_linker_inventory.py fails if a
registered linker goes uninventoried. So the number has exactly one
home that is checked, and these two call sites now defer to it instead
of keeping private copies that drift in silence.

This is the ONE FACT, TWO HOMES failure in its cheapest form: the
second home wins for whoever reads it, and nothing tells them it is
stale. Removing the copy is the fix; updating it is the workaround.

